you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  july is confirmed as earth's hottest  month ever recorded  we reveal the oil companies paying  negative tax on their north sea  operations  and meet the woman growing tea to save a  rainforest in paraguay  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  july 2021 was the hottest month ever  recorded on earth according to data  released by the united states the  national oceanic and atmospheric  administration described it as the  disturbing and disruptive path that  climate change has set for the globe  well let's talk to our correspondent  charlotte lomas now so charlotte an  uncomfortable milestone reached then in  july  it is anna this is according to data as  you mentioned there from noaa the  national oceanic and atmospheric  administration and they found that the  global surface temperature for july was  the hottest since records began in 1880  now the agency measured the combined  land and ocean surface temperature and  found it to be  0.93 degrees celsius above the 20th  century average of 15.8  degrees they also gave a breakdown  regionally and found that asia was  leading the way had the hottest july  ever and europe had its second hottest  july ever and experts have described  this as adding to the disturbing and  disruptive path that climate change has  set  charlotte thank you  well red alert heat warnings have been  issued across italy as southern europe  continues to experience a severe heat  wave so let's take a look at what's  happening today and this is the eu's  emergency monitoring system now it  forecasts fire danger levels based on  meteorological predictions and you can  see here that large areas of turkey  greece albania and southern italy as  well are dark purple now these areas are  most at risk of fire and it's the same  for north macedonia spain and portugal  as well as tunisia now if you look at  the red dots they show where they've  been active fires just in the last 24  hours  well while wildfires are a common  occurrence there's a very specific link  to our changing climate which is known  as the climate feedback loop now  increase global emissions lead to higher  temperatures which cause milder winters  and longer summers now these conditions  leave forests full of dried out and dead  vegetation which acts as fuel now that  means that man-made and natural fires  start easily and spread quickly  now when forests burn trees release  carbon dioxide and other greenhouse  gases into the atmosphere and that  increased amount of carbon accelerates  global warming  pushing temperatures up even further and  causing that cycle to continue  now in the town of tivoli east of rome  residents have been forced to evacuate  after a fire broke out in rome it's one  of hundreds of fires that are continuing  to rage across southern italy  and the fires are burning in the u.s too  these pictures show the extent of the  devastation in northern california the  dixie fire has burned around 75 percent  of the historical town of greenville  destroying hundreds of buildings and  forcing residents to flee their homes  and it's still threatening several small  towns in the sierra nevada with only 30  percent of that fire contained  meanwhile in turkey severe floods and  mudslides have killed 27 people and one  other person is still missing now more  than 1 700 people have been evacuated  from the black sea coastal provinces of  barton casta monu synop and samsung and  hundreds have been rescued by  helicopters aisha zarhid has been  looking at the reasons behind the severe  rainfall  turkey is on the front line of a  disaster triggered by the changing  climate  flooding in the northern region has  destroyed homes and claimed lives  thousands have been forced to evacuate  this is the most severe flood disaster  that i have ever seen both in terms of  area that was affected and its severity  despite suffering repeated extreme  weather events turkey remains the only  g20 country not to ratify the paris  climate agreement  a summer of record-breaking temperatures  contributed to this deluge  when air heats up it holds more moisture  so every time the temperature increases  by 1 degree celsius  moisture in the air can increase by 7  percent  this water has to go somewhere and  that's why we see increases in intense  rainstorms  when we look at the events that we are  having at the moment in turkey these are  events that  are not have been seen before certain  countries are in much worse shape like  bangladesh or india or pakistan all of  these countries  will have  worse problems than the average of the  world but nobody is spared  more events like this are inevitable  what's important to consider now is how  we prepare for them  aisha zahid sky news  in today's other climate news a glacier  on italy's side of mont blanc is at risk  of collapse which scientists say is a  result of climate change the planned  pancreas glacier reached its melting  point a decade ago and since then rising  temperatures have caused the ice mass to  move from its once secure position on  the mont blanc massive the glacier now  rests on a smoother part of the mountain  putting the valve ray valley below at  risk  footballer morton thorsby is switching  his shirt number in an effort to raise  awareness of climate change the  midfielder who plays for the italian  team sampdoria normally sports number 18  but has swapped it for a number two he  says the number represents the paris  agreement target of keeping global  temperatures well below two degrees  above pre-industrial levels  and earlier this year the uk government  launched a 500 million pound blue planet  fund to support countries on the front  line of climate change and today it's  been announced that 16.2 million pounds  from that fund will be invested in five  projects that focus on protecting the  ocean  however greenpeace's head of oceans says  the government isn't doing enough to  protect uk waters  we've been out at sea for the last few  months in the channel and inside our  marine protected areas we've seen  everything from illegal fishing to  destructive bottom trawling to super  trawlers over a hundred meters long the  uk if it wants to be a global leader it  wants to be seen to be a world leader in  ocean protection it has to do  the work at home to protect its  protected areas as well as the work that  it's doing overseas  some of the world's biggest oil  companies are currently paying negative  tax in the uk on some north sea  operations  data shows in the tax year 2019 to 20  exxon mobil received 117 million pounds  in total from hmrc  shell got 110 million pounds and bp  received 39 million and they're not  alone our climate correspondent hannah  thomas peter has more  this situation is possible because of a  uk tax policy that was actually brought  in around the time of the climate of the  paris climate change  agreement that allows oil companies  working in the north sea uh to claim  public money in order to help them with  the costs of decommissioning their rigs  and infrastructure uh and  tens and of millions of pounds hundreds  of millions actually have been paid uh  by hmrc to these big oil companies since  the tax policy  came into force um the uk oil and gas  authority actually estimates that the  total cost of decommissioning these rigs  as the uk advances towards its net zero  targets in 2050 is going to be about 51  billion pounds and because of this uk  tax policy the british taxpayer is on  the hook for nearly 40 percent of that  uh the treasury has estimated that the  cost to ex to the exchequer up until  about  uh 2065 is going to be some 18 billion  pounds so a lot of money at stake  campaigners say that this basically  amounts to the british taxpayer  subsidizing fossil fuels effectively uh  and that the uk  should in fact be assuming a kind of  moral and practical leadership position  in the months before it hosts the cop 26  uh climate change conference um but  we've had lots of responses from the  companies  involved and uh also from the treasury  and they've given us some statements  treasury spokesperson told sky news that  the uk oil and gas industry has paid  around 375 billion pounds in production  taxes to date relief for decommissioning  costs is a fundamental part of the uk's  tax system contributing to the safe  removal of oil oil and gas  infrastructure from our natural  environment while ensuring companies are  encouraged to invest in the uk and a  spokesperson from exxon  mobile said that over the lifetime of  the north sea we have been a major net  contributor to the tax revenues  generated by the basin and the recent  refunds simply represent a repayment of  some prior paid taxes some of our older  fields enter the decommissioning phase  of their life  similarly a spokesperson  for shell has said that decommissioning  is part of the life cycle of oil fields  this phase of work is heavily regulated  and subject to tax legislation that  enables tax relief the concept of  granting tax relief for genuine business  expenses is fundamental to regimes that  tax profits  and a spokesperson for bp said that the  data in question only covers the  extractive part of our business in the  uk our north sea business and bp has  contributed over 40 billion in taxes to  the uk government with respect to its  north sea businesses  we can get more details and comments  from the companies and the treasury on  our website just head to skynews.com  climate  the atlantic forest in south america was  once one of the largest rainforests in  the world covering more than 200 million  acres of land now just 20 percent  remains one woman who's working to  restore the area is fabiana benitez  she's part of a team of conservationists  who are growing tea to help biodiversity  there  so since i was a kid i want to be  involved in biodiversity  san rafael is one of the most important  atlantic forest remnant in paraguay and  we are facing  a lot of threats  from illegal logging wildfires hunting  encroachment  and other illegal activities  and how do we do this job using yerba  mate  yoramata is a native plant of the  atlantic forest it's part of our  identity as paraguayans and is globally  known as tea  [Music]  one hectare of these productive systems  is one hectare gained for the atlantic  forest and one hectare to generate  incomes for the rural and indigenous  communities and improve their  livelihoods  [Music]  well that's everything from us today but  don't forget to check out the weekly  climate show and in the latest episode  find out how climate change could affect  your sex life you can find that on the  sky news snapchat channel  and in this week's episode of  climatecast we look at what the world  and its leaders need to do to slow  climate change following the ipcc report  that's out now wherever you normally get  your podcast from thanks for watching  see you next week  [Music]

